In <29ej1h$n33@hpuerca.atl.hp.com>, sailer@atl.hp.com (Lee Sailer) writes:
>Another difference between threads (tasks) and processes that I'm not quite
>sure about has to do with scheduling.  As I understand it, all a process's
>tasks share that one process's timeslice.  On the other hand, forked
>process's each get a timeslice of their own.
>
>  If this is true, than a function devided into 5 processes might execute
>faster than the same function divided into 5 threads within one process,
>since the 5 process solution would get five times the cpu time.
>
>  lee


This is true only for user-level threads.  User-level thread libraries are 
cooperatively multitasked within a process' time-slice - the kernel doesn't
know anything about them.  A process only has a single thread of execution 
to work with.  With kernel-level threads OTOH, the thread is the unit of
scheduling.  In other words the scheduler doesn't even deal with processes
but just with threads.  In this case a process starting 5 threads would get
the same average cpu time as 5 single-threaded processes (assuming all the
other variables like priority and i/o are kept equal).


Zac 

