In article <29ej1h$n33@hpuerca.atl.hp.com>, sailer@atl.hp.com (Lee Sailer) writes:
|> Another difference between threads (tasks) and processes that I'm not quite
|> sure about has to do with scheduling.  As I understand it, all a process's
|> tasks share that one process's timeslice.  On the other hand, forked
|> process's each get a timeslice of their own.

You are wrong, at least in OSF/1 and Mach. A thread is the schedulable entity
and not the task/process. Thus 5 processes would get as much time as 5 threads 
as they would be scheduled in the same way. There is a small difference in that 
switching between 2 threads in the same process/task would not have to change 
address spaces whereas switching between two processes would so 5 threads may 
get even more time than 5 processes due to lower system overhead.

Simon.

