In <1993Oct11.170510.13690@mnemosyne.cs.du.edu> tgeorges@nyx.cs.du.edu (Thomas Georges) writes:
> "What's the advantage of using threads over fork/exec with signals?"

There is really only one true difference:  all threads within a process
share that process's state (e.g., global variables, open files, etc).  The
other differences, e.g., "forks are more expensive" and "scheduling is
different" are not architectural differences.  These, and other differences
mentioned on the net, vary among operating systems, and hardware.

Okay, given global shared state what can you do with it?  Here's a simple
use.  A client knows that there are 10 servers out there that can solve
its problem.  It spawns ten threads, each one doing an RPC.  The first
one that comes back sends a cancel message to the other nine.  Suppose you
want to keep 10 "calculators" busy each doing a partitioned part of, say,
a Mandelbrot calculation.  Each thread just pulls another chunk of the
problem out of a pool.  (Call it tuple-space and you just invented inverted
Linda. :-)

Correctly programming with threads is difficult.  There's a book on Modula-3
that has a great chapter on how to use threads; it was written by Andrew
Birrell and is a slight revision of a wonderful DEC technical paper.  The
book is worth it for just this chapter.
	/r$


