> > Yes, forking a process is much more expensive than spawning thread because
> > of the overhead.  To create a process, the system must set up the address
> > space and other system resources (memory pages, file descriptors, etc. etc..)
> > 
> > In a multithreaded process (Mach calls it "task"), threads within the same
> > process share all of the process's resources, so creating a thread is a
> > cheap operation: setting up the thread running context (program counter,
> > registers set, etc...).  It inherits the rest from the parent process.
> > And since threads share the process's resources, communication between them
> > can be done very naturally and efficiently with shared memory.
> > 
> O.K.  So how do you create a thread?

If you using the pthread package (pthread for POSIX threads).  There are a 
whole set of pthread calls: pthread_create, pthread_join, pthread_exit, ...

If you have access to it, there's a good chapter on pthreads in the
OSF/1 Application Programmer's Guide.  I don't know if DEC includes it
with the Alpha documentation.  OSF only publishes the reference documents 
and the User's Guide through Prentice Hall.
> 
> Also, what is the functional (NOT operational) difference between a thread and fork?

As the above description states, the thread
inherits much of its state from the parent process.  Basically, this means
that they share the same address space.  This has its ups and downs.

Ups:  less expensive to create because you don't have to initialize 
entire address space and easy to communicate back and forth because all
you have to do is write into a global address (if more than one thread is
writing to the same address, you may have simultaneous update issues).

Downs: Any interface that remembers the context of a call may have problems 
with threads.  If you don't one thread can make a call and another can get 
the first thread's context.  You need to use thread_safe versions of these
calls.  For example the random() call returns a fixed  series of random numbers
depending on the seed set in initstate().  If two threads are both calling 
random, they will each get only some of the numbers in the series.  The other
thread will get the missing numbers.  The random_r() allows you to specify a
pointer to a structure that gives each thread its own random series.  Obviously,
in this particular case, you might not care that you were getting an unpredictable
set of random numbers, but there are other state-sensitive calls that would
provide you with some bugs that are very tricky to find.

