tgeorges@nyx.cs.du.edu (Thomas Georges) writes:

>I just finished the DCE applications programming 3 day class and was 
>discussion some issues with a coworker.  He asked the question, "What's the
>advantage of using threads over fork/exec with signals?"

>Anyone care to post some advantages?  His thought was that the non-threded
>kernels would have lots of overhead that could be avoided by using
>fork and exec.

The two most obvious reasons are efficiency and convenience.

a) Fork is usually a fairly heavyweight operation.  Creating a thread
  should be fairly fast, making it feasible to create a new thread
  as needed.  Also, scheduling of threads within a process doesn't
  require a context switch, and there takes much less time than
  switching between two processes.

2) Because threads share an address space, data communication among threads
  is simpler.  Two communicate between processes you must go to
  some trouble to set up an IPC mechanism.  with threads, the data
  is simply there.
-- 
