In article <mauney.750363918@adm> mauney@adm.csc.ncsu.edu (Jon Mauney) writes:

>   a) Fork is usually a fairly heavyweight operation.  Creating a thread
>     should be fairly fast

Yes, forking a process is much more expensive than spawning thread because
of the overhead.  To create a process, the system must set up the address
space and other system resources (memory pages, file descriptors, etc. etc..)

In a multithreaded process (Mach calls it "task"), threads within the same
process share all of the process's resources, so creating a thread is a
cheap operation: setting up the thread running context (program counter,
registers set, etc...).  It inherits the rest from the parent process.
And since threads share the process's resources, communication between them
can be done very naturally and efficiently with shared memory.

>     .... Also, scheduling of threads within a process doesn't
>     require a context switch, and there takes much less time than
>     switching between two processes.

Well, whether context switching is required for thread scheduling depends
on what kind of threads we're talking about.  If it's kernel thread,
switching between threads DOES require a context switch.  If it's
user-level thread, then the user-thread run-time library schedules the
threads, and the kernel doesn't know about it.  To the OS kernel, it's only
one process.  It's the runtime library that implements handles user-level
threads. 
  And since the kernel doesn't know about user-level threads, a context
switch means all the user threads in the same process get swapped out (or
in).  Also for the same reason, user-level threads within the same process
cannot make use of multiple processors at once.

/Thuan



